<<~ ahu slot=`#/head`>>

! The face intent plane, 2026 ~ what the field says about a channel of intent

The operator un-asked the daemon doc on 2026-10-20 and kept one point: the operator and the AIs need a channel of intent through each face's fleet of device vessels. A Sonnet pair read the field in two traded waves: YIN on multi-device fleets, YANG on intent channels between humans and AI agents. Each claim reads MEASURED (read in a source on 2026-10-20) or LEAD (inference). The model the operator approved rests at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-intent-plane`>>.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/findings`>>

!! Findings

# ''The field splits what the daemon doc fused'' (both waves, MEASURED). The roster (Keyhive: a person as a group of device keys; Keybase's sigchain); shared state; per-device delivery (Matrix to-device, its transaction id scoped to one device and one endpoint); the signed request (a UCAN invocation, its task id a content hash of subject, command, arguments and nonce); the outcome (a receipt whose `cause` names the request; Temporal's recorded results); the authority (a delegation chain whose revocations append and arrive out of order). Two answerers for one summons follows from one mutable entry holding request and answer.
# ''No source elects an answerer for "any device"'' (both, MEASURED). MCP, A2A, UCAN, Temporal and Restate each assume one executor or one ordering server. WhatsApp encrypts a separate copy for every device in both device lists, and its server holds nothing after delivery. Matrix delivers exactly once per device, with `*` for every known device: exactly-once delivery, never exactly-once action. Syncthing's one deterministic device tie-break reads the wall clock first and the device id only last.
# ''A lease cannot give mutual exclusion'' (YIN, MEASURED in Kleppmann 2016): a paused holder acts past its lease; the safe fix needs a fencing token at the effect and consensus, which a CRDT fleet lacks. A claim can steer effort and never carry safety; safety lives in idempotent or fenced effects.
# ''Lowest-id reads, never elects'' (YIN, correcting YANG's first wave). A partitioned device cannot know the other claimants; a winner rule picks the canonical record among acts that already happened, and the loser's record stays visible, as Syncthing keeps a conflict copy.
# ''Temporal and Restate'' (YANG, MEASURED): one open execution per workflow id, and "Use Existing" hands a second asker the open run; Restate's awakeable is a one-shot id an outside party resolves, the shape of a human approval. Both rest on one ordering server.
# ''No clockless claim expiry exists in the field'' (both, LEAD): the pair proposes release, takeover and causal voids; staleness stays a device's local suspicion.
# ''The log belongs to the face'' (both converged, LEAD grounded in the repo's ontology): a vessel-held bag would need a second roster and break one owner per registry; a face plane derived from the tag syncs to every device the face admits.
# ''The far agent'' (YANG, LEAD shaped by UCAN and XRPC): its own key and a narrow delegation; a relay seat admits its signed invocation and countersigns only the admission; the answering device signs the receipt.

<<~/ahu>>

